#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "cd699f35-88bd-57b5-8413-f26ee0752f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-10130" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{5}{33}x^{4},\\begin{vmatrix}-1 < x < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf the probability density function of $Y = X^2$ is given by\n${g(y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 < y < 1 \\\\ h(y),\\end{vmatrix}1 \\le y < 4; 0, \\mid otherwise$\nfind the function $h(y)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y = X^2$ over the interval $-1 < x < 2$.\n\n![](images/c9/c98b2d947486e4666380dc8412c1b64a8518c84e39372052602a9402ab3d4211.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $-1 \\lt x < 1$. On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0 \\lt y \\lt 1$.\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $1 \\leq x \\lt 2$. On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $1 \\leq y \\lt 4$.\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval\n\n$$\n1 \\leq y \\lt 4\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for\n\n$$\n1 \\leq y \\lt 4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(X^{2} \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(\\sqrt{X^{2}} \\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= P(\\mid X \\mid \\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= F_{X}(\\sqrt{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used\n\n$$\n\\mid X \\mid = X\n$$\n\nfor $X > 0$.\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for\n\n$$\n1 \\leq y \\lt 4\n$$\n\nwe differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot f_{X}(\\sqrt{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot f_{X}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\frac{5}{33}(\\sqrt{y})^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\frac{5}{33}y^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{66}y^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nh(y) = \\dfrac{5}{66}\\,y^{3/2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176072 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f695215-606d-5c8b-87d2-dcf08bdc84e3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Two-to-One Transformations to Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "501ad713-9e9d-53d7-ac8e-397edd64bee1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9161" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{1}{24}(x + 1)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}-3 < x < 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = \\mid X \\mid$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the transformation\n\n$$\nY = \\mid X \\mid\n$$\n\nis two-to-one over the interval $-3\\lt x \\lt 3$.\n\n![](images/8b/8bc657a6b1b0defb0857f665ae48353862f06e0145bbc22ad3dac906da720380.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-3\\lt x \\lt 3$, we have that\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\lt 3\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y < 3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(\\mid X \\mid \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(-y \\le X \\le y) \\\\\n&= F_{X}(y) - F_{X}(-y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y < 3\n$$\n\nwe differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= F_{X}^{′}(y) - (-1)F_{X}^{′}(-y) \\\\\n&= F_{X}^{′}(y) + F_{X}^{′}(-y) \\\\\n&= f_{X}(y) + f_{X}(-y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= f_{X}(y) + f_{X}(-y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}(y + 1)^{2} + \\frac{1}{24}(-y + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}[(y^{2} + 2y + 1) + (y^{2} - 2y + 1)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}(2y^{2} + 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(y^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{12}(y^{2} + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y < 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$"}]}
